  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Complicated forecast for next week.\n\nForecast for Halloween and this weekend is pretty\nstraightforward. Mid to upper level clouds are going to expand \nin coverage in the region and temperatures will be closer to \nseasonal norms. High temperatures for the next few days will be \nin the upper 50s to upper 60s with lows in the upper 30s to \nmid-40s. \n\nAfter Monday, however, the forecast becomes a headache. A mid-level \nlow over the Pacific Northwest is expected to split in two with a \nnorthern and southern piece. The southern piece is expected to \nincrease in size and strength as it passes through the Rocky \nMountains, consuming a weakening wave further to its south. The \nnorthern component is also forecast to move eastward along the \nCanadian-American border. Both of these synoptic scale features are \nlikely to be over the Great Plains by around Tuesday. At this point, \nmodel guidance enters serious disagreement on timing and impacts to \nour area. Two (maybe even three) meridional aligned lows may \nend up forming across the plains and how they interact with one\nanother will determine its affects on the Mid-Atlantic. The \nproposed scenarios are diverse as models suggest one stronger \nlow overpowering its neighbor and redirecting its path and \nenergy around it. These lows are still projected to continue \neastward which suggests a chance of rain for the area, but it is\nfar too early and unclear to know how much rain and for how \nlong. Surface low pressure systems associated with this mid- \nlevel pattern could also send warm and cold fronts through \ntowards the end of next week but the temperature forecast also \nremains uncertain until there can be a better model consensus.",
